# AGENTS.md: {{ service_name }}

{{ description }}. Part of **FamilyMeal**, a family meal planner: an LLM builds a 3–7 day
batch-cooking menu from a recipe catalog, recipes are imported by URL, and a shopping list
is built from the accepted plan.

This file is for coding agents (Claude Code, Codex, Cursor, ...) and humans alike: hard
rules here, details in `docs/`. Cross-service rules, contracts and ADRs live in the
**meal-platform** repository; read its `AGENTS.md` before changing anything that crosses a
service boundary.

## Commands

| Task | Command |
|---|---|
| Install deps and hooks | `make install` |
| Everything CI runs, except the Trivy scans | `make check` |
| Lint / autofix | `make lint` / `make fmt` |
| Types (pyright strict) | `make typecheck` |
| Layer contracts (import-linter) | `make imports` |
| Tests | `make test` or `uv run pytest tests/test_x.py -k name` |
| Tests without `integration` ones{% if with_database or with_cache %} (no Docker){% endif %} | `make test-unit` |
{%- if with_database %}
| Apply migrations | `make migrate` (`DB__URL` from `.env`; deploys run `python -m {{ package_name }} migrate`) |
| New migration from models | `make migration name=add_recipes`, then review the file |
{%- endif %}
| Run locally | `cp .env.example .env && make run` → http://localhost:{{ http_port }}/docs |
| Pull template updates | `make update-template` |

Always run `make check` before saying a task is done. Never add a dependency with pip;
use `uv add <pkg>` (or `uv add --dev`) so `uv.lock` stays the source of truth.

## Layout and layering

```
src/{{ package_name }}/
  domain/          entities, value objects, domain errors, ports (typing.Protocol)
  application/     use cases: one module per use case, depends on domain only
  infrastructure/  adapters implementing ports: HTTP clients, LLM
{%- if with_database %}
    db/            engine, sessions, ORM `Base` (models.py), repositories, migrations/
{%- endif %}
{%- if with_cache %}
    cache/         `ValkeyCache` (TTL on every write, Valkey down = miss); feature caches wrap it
{%- endif %}
  api/             routers, request/response schemas, providers (dependencies.py), errors.py
  observability/   logging, tracing, metrics (do not bypass)
  settings.py      pydantic-settings, env vars only
  main.py          composition root: create_app() and the lifespan
tests/             mirrors src/; fixtures in tests/conftest.py, shared fakes in tests/fakes/
docs/              architecture, stack and conventions (see Docs below)
```

Dependency direction: `api → application → domain ← infrastructure`, checked by `make imports`.

- `domain` imports nothing from FastAPI, SQLAlchemy, httpx2, Redis, Ollama, `observability`
  or `settings`.
- `application` talks to the outside world only through ports defined in `domain`.
- Wiring (which adapter implements which port) happens in `main.py` and the providers in
  `api/dependencies.py`. Routes get everything through typed dependencies (`SettingsDep`,
  `HttpClientDep`{% if with_database %}, `DbSessionDep`{% endif %}{% if with_cache %}, `CacheDep`{% endif %}, a use case's own alias).
{%- if with_database %}
- Database: repositories in `infrastructure/db/` on the request's `DbSessionDep` session (one
  transaction per request); they never commit. Tables change only via migrations, which
  must work with the release still running (add first; drop or rename in a later release).
{%- endif %}
{%- if with_cache %}
- The cache is never the source of truth: cache-aside through a feature port, a TTL on
  every write, no personal data in keys.
{%- endif %}
- The LLM is just another port (`domain`) with an Ollama adapter (`infrastructure`).
  LLM output is always parsed into a Pydantic model; never pass raw LLM text into the domain.

## Where does new code go?

| I need to... | Put it in |
|---|---|
| a new business concept or rule | `domain/<feature>/`: entity or value object (frozen dataclass) |
| talk to something external | port in `domain/<feature>/ports.py`, adapter in `infrastructure/` |
| a new operation the service performs | `application/<feature>/<verb>_<noun>.py`, one use case class |
| a new HTTP endpoint | `api/<feature>.py` (router + schemas), provider in `api/dependencies.py`, `include_router` in `main.py` |
| a new config value | `settings.py` (a sub-model per adapter) + `.env.example` |
| a client, pool or other resource with a lifetime | opened in `lifespan` (`main.py`), field on `AppState`, provider in `api/dependencies.py` |
{%- if with_database %}
| a DB table | `infrastructure/db/models.py` + `make migration name=...` |
| a repository | `infrastructure/db/<feature>.py`, implementing ports from `domain/<feature>/ports.py` |
{%- endif %}
{%- if with_cache %}
| cache a slow lookup | port in `domain/<feature>/ports.py`, adapter wrapping `ValkeyCache` in `infrastructure/cache/<feature>.py` |
{%- endif %}
| a fake for tests | `tests/fakes/<feature>.py` |

## Code rules

- Python {{ python_version }}, full type hints, pyright strict must pass. No `Any` in signatures
  of domain or application code; no `# type: ignore`, only `# pyright: ignore[<rule>]` with a reason.
- Pydantic models at the boundaries (API, LLM output, other services' payloads, settings);
  frozen dataclasses inside the domain. API schemas subclass `ApiModel`.
- Async all the way for I/O. No blocking calls (`requests`, `time.sleep`) in async code.
- Errors: domain and application raise the classes in `domain/errors.py` (or subclasses).
  The central handlers in `api/errors.py` turn them into problem-details responses; routes
  never catch them to map them and never raise `HTTPException` for them. Adapters convert
  library exceptions into domain errors. Log an error once, where it is handled.
- Settings: `get_settings()` only in the composition root; components get their slice;
  secrets are `SecretStr`.

## Observability (non-negotiable: a primary goal of this project)

- Logging: `from {{ package_name }}.observability import get_logger`;
  `_logger = get_logger(__name__)`. Never `print`, never `logging.getLogger` (ruff bans it).
- Log events are dotted snake_case names with key-value context, not prose:
  `_logger.info("recipe.imported", recipe_id=..., source="json-ld")`. No f-strings in messages.
- Never log secrets, tokens or full LLM prompts with personal data.
- Incoming HTTP and outgoing httpx2 calls are traced automatically. Add a manual span for every
  use case and every LLM call: `with get_tracer(__name__).start_as_current_span("plan.generate"):`.
  Put useful ids on spans as attributes (`span.set_attribute("recipe.id", ...)`).
- Other services are called over HTTP through the shared httpx2 client (`HttpClientDep`),
  which carries the trace context: one user action must be one trace across all services.
- Business metrics through `get_meter(__name__)` (counters/histograms), names like
  `recipes.imported`, with low-cardinality attributes only.

## Testing

- Test the requirement, not the implementation: happy path, edge cases, failures and
  invariants. If a requirement is unclear, stop and ask. Coverage finds gaps; it is not the goal.
- Domain and application: fast unit tests with in-memory fakes for ports, no network.
- API: the `client` fixture (`TestClient`) with providers overridden by fakes.
{%- if with_database %}
- Database: `@pytest.mark.integration` tests against PostgreSQL in a testcontainer;
  `db_session` for repositories, `db_client` (not `TestClient`, which runs the app in
  another event loop than the test's asyncpg connections) for HTTP tests.
{%- endif %}
{%- if with_cache %}
- Cache: adapter tests against Valkey in a testcontainer (`cache_settings`, `cache_client`),
  marked `integration`; never hand an async fixture's client to an app under `TestClient`.
{%- endif %}
- Observability is asserted with the `span_exporter`, `log_exporter` and `metric_reader` fixtures.
- Warnings are errors in tests. Fix the cause instead of filtering it.
- Every bug fix starts with a failing test.

## Boundaries for agents

- Contracts: when the HTTP API changes, update its OpenAPI copy in meal-platform
  `contracts/openapi/` in the same change.
- Ask before: adding a new runtime dependency, changing a contract, changing CI or Dockerfile,
  adding a `.trivyignore` entry, touching anything in `observability/` beyond adding helpers.
- Never: commit secrets or `.env`, disable or skip tests to get green, weaken pyright or ruff
  settings, edit `.copier-answers.yml` by hand, ignore a Trivy finding that has a fix.
- Files that come from meal-service-template (CI, Dockerfile, observability, tooling config)
  should be improved in the template and pulled with `make update-template`.

